Let a DTACK end the bus cycle during the RESET instruction.

WAITSTATES tests RESET_OUT_I before DTACK_In, so while the core is executing a
RESET instruction it ignores an acknowledgement and holds the bus cycle open
for the instruction's whole 124-clock assertion.  A real MC68010 does not: the
RESET instruction runs no bus cycle of its own, and a prefetch already in
flight terminates normally.

The Sun-2 hits this at its very first instruction.  The VME boot PROM executes
`reset' at 0xEF00CC, immediately before _hardreset's body:

    ef00cc:  4e70            reset
    ef00ce:  3e7c 0003       moveaw #3,%sp
    ef00d2:  4e7b f001       movec %sp,%dfc

The prefetch of 0xEF00D2 is in flight when RESET_OUT_I asserts.  The machine
acknowledges it at its C_S4, DTACK_In duly latches low on the next falling
edge -- and WAITSTATES stays 1 anyway, so AS is never negated.  Measured, both
cycles side by side, the only difference being this signal:

    ef00d0  DTACK_In=0 WAITSTATES=0 SLICE=010 RESET_OUT=0   -> cycle ends
    ef00d2  DTACK_In=0 WAITSTATES=1 SLICE=001 RESET_OUT=1   -> cycle never ends

Holding AS asserted leaves the Sun-2's bus timing chain counting, and at C_S8
its protection check fires against a page map entry software has not written
yet.  The CPU takes the bus error, pushes the frame onto a stack in the same
unwritten map, faults again and double-faults.  On real hardware every map
entry is zero at power-up, so this is deterministic: the machine halts before
it writes its front panel, which is exactly how it presents on a board.

Moving the test below the three legitimate terminations preserves what the
comment says it is for -- with nothing acknowledging, the '1' is still returned
before BUS_FLT is reached, so there is still no bus fault during RESET -- while
letting a cycle that was acknowledged finish.

--- a/68K10/wf68k10_bus_interface.vhd	2026-08-21 17:13:25.507455314 +0200
+++ b/68K10/wf68k10_bus_interface.vhd	2026-08-21 17:13:49.952174348 +0200
@@ -556,10 +556,10 @@
     OPCODE_TO_CORE <= OBUFFER;
 
     WAITSTATES <= '0' when T_SLICE /= S3 else
-                  '1' when RESET_OUT_I = '1' else -- No bus fault during RESET instruction.
                   '0' when SYNCn = '0' else -- For synchronous bus cycles.
                   '0' when DTACK_In = '0' else -- For asynchronous bus cycles.
                   '0' when ADR_IN_P(19 downto 16) = x"F" and AVEC_In = '0' else -- Interrupt acknowledge space cycle.
+                  '1' when RESET_OUT_I = '1' else -- No bus fault during RESET instruction.
                   '0' when BUS_FLT = '1' else -- In case of a bus error;
                   '0' when RESET_CPU_I = '1' else '1'; -- A CPU reset terminates the current bus cycle.
 
